rankin@eql.caltech.edu (Pat Rankin) writes:

>In article <1993Feb5.131140.9793@ousrvr.oulu.fi>,\
> sn@phoenix.oulu.fi (Sami Nieminen) writes...
>>   This is a fairly short question: What is the file RECOVER.EXE used
>> for ? Crash recovery ?
>
>     If you compile with INSURANCE #defined (which is the default) and
>you play with the `checkpoint' option enabled (which is also the default
>unless the `INSURANCE' code is suppressed), then each time you change
>levels during play, sufficient information to reconstruct a save file
>is written to disk.  The `recover' utility reads all those individual
>level files left behind after a crash and writes out a save file which
>can be used to restore the game that was in progress at the time of the
>crash.  [At least in theory...]


This is a great improvement over no recovery possibility at all.  Now
that there's recovery code, and even a binary option for checkpointing,
why not extend the code and allow checkpointing every X turns, making
``checkpoint'' an integer setting?
And/or, add a new command to the game that means "checkpoint right now",
which would be very desirable to do right after you've gotten a bit lucky
or won some hard-earned treasure which you want to make sure you don't
lose.

-jim


